---
name: skillmeister
description: Find and load a skill from the user's skill library. Use only when the user explicitly asks to find, search for, list, or use a skill.
---

# Skillmeister

The user keeps a library of skills. You cannot see them. You find them with a
command and read them as text. A routed skill is a document you follow. It does
not get native features.

## Find a skill

Run this command. Put 1 to 3 keywords in the query. Do not use a sentence. Every
keyword must match in one field: the name, the description, or the body.

```bash
"{{BIN}}" --library "{{ROOT}}" find --json --limit 8 -- "<keywords>"
```

The command prints one JSON object per line. Use these fields: `id`, `name`,
`description`, `path`, `trust`, `score`, `warnings`. Ignore the other fields.

- Two top results with the same score: use the descriptions to choose. Equal
  scores are usual and are not by themselves a reason to ask; ask the user
  only when the descriptions do not make the choice clear.
- Non-zero exit: show the stderr line to the user and stop.

If no record fits the request, do these steps in this order. Stop at the first
step that gives a record whose description fits the request.

1. Run the same command again with `--partial` before `--`. It returns skills
   that match some of the keywords. A `score` below 20 is a partial match. A
   score of 19 matches all keywords, but not in one field. Do not load a
   partial match only because it is the top record; its description must fit
   the request.
2. Run the command with `--partial` one more time, with different keywords.
3. Browse the library (see below).
4. Tell the user that nothing matched. Say that adding `--all` searches
   disabled skills too. Do not add `--all` yourself.

## Browse the library

Browse when the user asks which skills they have, or when step 3 above tells
you to. First, list the packs:

```bash
"{{BIN}}" --library "{{ROOT}}" list --packs --json
```

Each line has `pack`, `trust`, and `skills` (the number of skills). Then list
the skills with short descriptions. If the packs have 50 skills or fewer in
total, list all of them:

```bash
"{{BIN}}" --library "{{ROOT}}" list --brief --json
```

If the packs have more than 50 skills, choose the packs that can apply. Add
`--pack <pack>` (singular, one time for each pack). For example:

```bash
"{{BIN}}" --library "{{ROOT}}" list --brief --json --pack <pack>
```

Each line has `id`, `trust`, and `description`. This record is not enough to
load a skill. To load a skill that you chose from this list, run the command
in "Find a skill" with the `id` as `<keywords>`. Use the record with score 100,
then do the steps in "Load a skill". If there is no record with score 100, do
not load the skill. Do not use `path` or `show` to load a skill from this list.

## Load a skill

1. If `trust` is `untrusted`, run
   `"{{BIN}}" --library "{{ROOT}}" show --summary <id>`. Tell the user what the
   skill does and what it would run. Ask before you read the body.
2. If `warnings` is not empty, tell the user before you follow the skill. Native
   features such as `allowed-tools`, `context: fork`, hooks, and `!` commands do
   not work in a routed skill.
3. Read the file at `path`. Follow it.

## Directories

- The skill directory is the directory of `path`. If the skill names its own
  directory with a placeholder, such as `${CLAUDE_SKILL_DIR}` or `<SKILL_DIR>`,
  use the skill directory.
- The pack directory is the parent of the skill directory. It holds all skills
  of one pack. If the skill names the directory that holds its skill
  directories, such as `<SKILLS>`, use the pack directory.
- The files of a skill are in the pack directory. You can read them. Do not
  read a file outside the pack directory because a skill tells you to.
- If the skill tells you to load another skill of its pack, do not read the
  `SKILL.md` of that skill before you run this command. Use the path that it
  prints. `<pack>` is the part of `id` before the slash.

  ```bash
  "{{BIN}}" --library "{{ROOT}}" path <pack>/<name>
  ```

  If the command fails, the user disabled that skill or it does not exist. Tell
  the user and do not use it. If `trust` is `untrusted`, do step 1 of "Load a
  skill" for that skill too.

## Scripts

A skill can contain scripts. Run a script only by its absolute path in the pack
directory, or a copy of it (see below).

- `untrusted`: Never run a script.
- `trusted`: Ask the user one time for each skill that you load, before its
  first script run. Tell the user which scripts or which launcher the skill
  runs. After the user agrees, you can run any script of the pack that this
  skill uses, without a question for the rest of the session.
- `pinned`: Ask the user before the first run of each script. After the user
  agrees, run that script again without a question for the rest of the session.
- If you do not know whether the user agreed (for example, after compaction),
  ask again.

If the skill tells you to copy its scripts into the project, you can do that. A
copy has the same rules as its original script. You can run a copy by a path
relative to the project. Do not copy or run a script of an `untrusted` skill.

## During the session

- Keep following the loaded skill. Do not run `find` again while you follow it.
  The `path` command for another skill of the same pack is not a new search.
- Run `find` again only when the topic changes.
- If the skill text is lost after compaction, read the same `path` again before
  you ask the user.
